¶
{{smaller block|The ''workability'' of compulsory notification would depend on the general practitioners. {{right|''Lancet'', 1890, II. 21.}}}}

'''workable''' (wėr′ka̤-bl), ''a.'' [< ''work'' + ''-able.''] '''1.''' That can be worked, or that is worth working: as, a ''workable'' mine; ''workable'' coal. {{smaller|The term ''workable'', as applied to coal, has two meanings: one refers to the maximum limit of depth, the other to the minimum limit of thickness of the bed or beds. In the Report of the English Royal Commission appointed in 1866, the limit of workable depth was taken as 4,000 feet, that of thickness at 1 foot. But no coal has yet been worked to so great a depth as that, and it has only very rarely happened that a seam of less than 2 feet in thickness has been actually mined.}}
{{smaller block|Clay . . . soft and ''workable''. {{right|''Ascham'', Toxophilus, ii.}}
I apprehend that the Commissioners [the English of 1866] placed the limit of thickness as low as 12 inches because their inquiries were not in that connection directed to the question what amount of coal would ultimately be found commercially ''workable;'' it was the simple physical limits which they were chiefly regarding. {{right|''Marshall'', Coal: its Hist. and Uses, p. 307.}}}}
'''2.''' Practicable; feasible: as, a ''workable'' scheme for lighting the streets.—'''3.''' Capable of being stirred or influenced.
{{smaller block|These have nimble feet, forward affections, hearts ''workable'' to charity. {{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, II. 410.}}}}
'''4.''' Capable of being set at work.
{{smaller block|At the time of taking the last census there were very nearly seven millions of wives and children of a ''workable'' age still unoccupied. {{right|''Mayhew'', London Labour and London Poor, II. 358.}}}}

'''workableness''' (wėr′ka̤-bl-nes), ''n.'' Practicableness; feasibility.
{{smaller block|That fair trial which alone can test the ''workableness'' of any new scheme of social life. {{right|''J. S. Mill'', Socialism.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
¶
'''workaday''' (wėrk′a̤-dā), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [Formerly also ''workyday''. Cf. ''workday''.] '''I.†''' ''n.'' A working-day.
{{smaller block|Trade, I cashier thee till to-morrow; friend Onion, for thy sake I finish this ''workiday''.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Case is Altered, iv. 3.}}
We find a great Deference paid to Saturday Afternoon, above the other ''worky-Days'' of the Week.
{{right|''Bourne’s Pop. Antiq.'' (1777), p. 145.}}}}
'''II.''' ''a.'' Working-day; relating to workdays; plodding; toiling.
{{smaller block|<poem>Your face shall be tann’d
Like a sailor’s ''worky-day'' hand.</poem>
{{right|''Middleton and Rowley'', Spanish Gypsy, iv. 1.}}
''Work-a-day'' humanity.
{{right|''Dickens'', Uncommercial Traveller, iv.}}
This is a ''workaday'', practical world, and . . . we must face things as they are. {{right|''The Century'', XXXIX. 630.}}}}

'''work-bag''' (wėrk′bag), ''n.'' A small bag of some textile material, formerly carried by women, and used to contain their needlework. The term was often used for the reticule.
{{smaller block|The lawful fine of the pledged ''work-bag'' of the king’s wife. {{right|''O’Curry'', Anc. Irish, II. xxiv.}}}}

'''work-basket''' (wėrk′bȧs″ket), ''n.'' A basket used by women either to hold the implements for sewing, as needles, thread, scissors, or thimble, in which case the basket is small, or to hold partly made garments, articles needing repair, etc., for which use the basket is large and has a wide opening.
{{smaller block|On the table is . . . Elizabeth’s ''workbasket''.
{{right|''Rhoda Broughton'', Alas, xxxiv.}}}}

'''work-box''' (wėrk′boks), ''n.'' A box used by women to hold their materials for sewing and the

readings.c1s3:
needlework itself when not too bulky.
{{smaller block|<poem>Here, lately shut, that ''work-box'' lay;
There stood your own embroidery frame.</poem>
{{right|''F. Locker'', The Castle in the Air.}}}}

'''workday''' (wėrk′dā), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [< ME. ''werkdai'', ''werkedei'', ''werkedai'', ''werrkedah'', workday, working-day, < AS. ''weorc-dæg'' (= G. ''werk-tag'', ''werkel-tag'' = Icel. ''verkdagr''); as ''work'' + ''day''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''n.'' A working-day; a week-day.
{{smaller block|<poem>For a-pon the ''werkeday''
Men be so bysy in vche way,
So that for here ocupacyone
They leue myche of here deuocyone.</poem>
{{right|''Myrc'', Instructions for Parish Priests (E. E. T. S.), l. 1005.}}}}
'''II.''' ''a.'' Of or pertaining to a working-day or working-days.
{{smaller block|Allow me my friends, my freedom, my rough companions, in their ''work-day'' clothes. {{right|''Thackeray'', Philip, vi.}}}}

'''worked-off''' (wėrkt′ôf″), ''a.'' In ''printing'', noting a form of type from which a required edition has been printed.

'''worker''' (wėr′kėr), ''n.'' [< ME. *''worker'', ''worcher''; < ''work'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' One who or that which works; a laborer; a toiler; a performer; a doer.
{{smaller block|False apostles, deceitful ''workers''. {{right|2 Cor. xi. 13.}}
<poem>Men, my brothers, men the ''workers'', ever reaping something new:
That which they have done but earnest of the things that they shall do.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Locksley Hall.}}
With co-partnership between employer and employed, the ''worker'' would feel he was more nearly the equal of the capitalist. {{right|''N. A. Rev.'', CXLII. 615.}}}}

